  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread smoke continues to degrade air quality and\n   visibility driven by regional wildfire activity. \n\n-  Elevated to near-critical fire weather continues along and\n   south of the I-90 corridor through Monday. \n\n-  Gradual cooling trend by middle of next week as showers return\n   to the forecast. \n\nThe Northern Rockies will remain under the influence of a dry\nwesterly flow aloft through early next week. High pressure will\nremain centered across the Great Basin and Four Corners states,\nwith low pressure systems passing to the north in Canada. This\npattern will continue to bring elevated fire weather concerns due\nto hot, dry, and breezy conditions. The worst fire weather is\nexpected along and east of the divide in southwest Montana, where\nnear-critical fire weather is forecast by Monday. Future shifts \nwill closely monitor the need for another round of Red Flag \nWarnings on Monday. Smoke from regional wildfires will continue \nto bring air quality impacts and reduced visibility for at least \nthe next 24 hours. \n\nA gradual moistening and slight cooling trend is expected by\nTuesday next week. Then, there is modest confidence for increased\nprecipitation chances and colder temperatures by Wednesday into \nThursday. The best chances for cooler and wetter conditions \nremains confined to areas north and along the Canadian Border into\nthe Flathead National Forest closer to the cold core of an \nincoming trough, where higher moisture availability and lifting \nmechanisms are expected.",
